title: "Sharp total variation bounds from spherical rearrangement"
authors:
  - family-names: Zweiman
    given-names: Henry
date-released: 2026-09-16
version: "1.13"
url: "https://github.com/Hwzw/algebra-number-theory-drafts/tree/main/papers/P47-sobolev-variation"
abstract: "Sharp all-order Hilbert total variation bounds via spherical rearrangement, with a measure-endpoint theorem and full local continuation to exponents near two, and the complete all-order measure-endpoint symmetry and extremizer classification, with an explicit fourth-order constant. Also proves all-order midpoint symmetry and height-extremizer uniqueness for every finite exponent. Also proves the full variation statement on an order-dependent interval immediately above p=1. Also proves finite nondegenerate switching and simple residual zeros for every finite-exponent variation maximizer, with an explicit necessary stability matrix. Also excludes two-switch variation maximizers at every finite exponent and in every derivative order at least three, without symmetry assumptions. Also excludes midpoint-symmetric three-switch maximizers and derives a necessary dilation-response condition for all remaining three-switch candidates. Includes an explicit rationally certified counterexample to the arbitrary derivative-weight extension, with persistence under smoothing. Also proves an explicit two-interval length concentration bound for order-three variation maximizers when 1<p<3. Proves the full order-three variation identity and complete equality classification for every finite p>1, and the constant identity at p=infinity. Completes the uniform-endpoint equality classification with an explicit four-piece third derivative, thereby proving the order-three conjecture at every exponent with the measure interpretation at p=1. Also proves all-order uniform-endpoint saturation for every variation maximizer, with null zero sets, endpoint residual normalization and a strict mean-parameter bound; finite simple switching and exactly n+r-1 residual zeros follow. Gives an implicit critical-point count bound uniform over all 1<p<=infinity for each fixed n>=3. AI-assisted preprint, not independently verified."
